I was surprised by this game, namely how well the platforming plays and controls. I came to this game for the story, especially since "A group of jumping blocks with their own strengths and weaknesses learn about the importance of friendship in a hostile, unpredictable world" sounds almost like a parody of art games that can't escape their own 'gamey' nature. And you can tell there was a small team working on it when there's a link to the lead developers' Twitter page, so I didn't expect the actual gameplay to be that involving.
Nevertheless, the story is good. Very good, and it wouldn't work without gameplay interactions. Both narrative devices- both the Stephen Merchant-esque narration that brings humour and character to faceless, silent programming systems, as well as the mechanics themselves. Things that would be otherwise included purely to design the puzzles become methaphorical story elements. When, for example, the platforms are collapsing under Thomas, forcing him to run to safety, the world itself is working against him, and he has to keep moving forward to stay alive. The simplified interactions with other characters range from optimistic (
) to downright saucy (
). If there's still any argument about games creating emotional beats from gameplay, keep in mind that most of the character moments I described involve nothing more than holding down the 'D' button to move from one end of the screen to another. And this made some people come to tears.
This game's worth a look as a talking point for the potential artistic power of video games, as an engaging character piece, and as a fun platformer on top of all that.

Been playing this game for a while, really enjoyed it but this game sure need some improvement imo:
- no story mission, every city you only have one mission which is ally or eliminate other faction. Other than that there is no other campaign mission. Though there's some kind of other side quest (electricity, water, etc), overall there's only one mission and it gets boring after couple of city;
- factions won't carry relationship status, let's say I eliminate certain faction during the campaign, then when I reach a new city and that faction is already in the city, the should carry that relationship status over to the new city.

